# ESP32 Keypad Temperature Monitor

Interrupt-driven ESP32 temperature monitor: DS18B20 + I2C LCD + 4x4 keypad + LEDs, built with ESP-IDF and FreeRTOS.



**Live simulation:** <https://wokwi.com/projects/477404738874159105>

## What it does

- Continuously shows the ambient temperature on a 16x2 LCD.
- Press **5**: Blue LED turns on, LCD shows `BLUE LED ON`, then `BLUE LED OFF` after 3 s.
- Press **D**: Red LED turns on, LCD shows `RED LED ON`, then `RED LED OFF` after 3 s.
- After each cycle the display returns to live temperature. Sensor readings never stop.

## How it works

```
keypad column pin --ISR--> task notification --> keypad_task --queue--> ui_task --> LEDs + LCD
sensor_task --------- shared temperature value ---------------------------^
```

- **ISR:** minimal. It masks the interrupt and notifies `keypad_task`. No scanning or delays inside.
- **keypad_task:** debounces, scans the matrix, and sends the key to a FreeRTOS queue.
- **ui_task:** owns the LCD and LEDs, and reacts to keys from the queue.
- **sensor_task:** reads the DS18B20 in the background using `vTaskDelay`, so it never blocks.

## Hardware / pin map

| Part | ESP32 pin |
|---|---|
| DS18B20 data (4.7 kΩ pull-up to 3V3) | GPIO 4 |
| LCD I2C (address 0x27) | SDA 21, SCL 22 |
| Keypad rows R1-R4 | 13, 14, 27, 26 |
| Keypad columns C1-C4 | 25, 33, 32, 23 |
| Blue LED (220 Ω) | GPIO 18 |
| Red LED (220 Ω) | GPIO 19 |

## Build and run

Requirements: VS Code, PlatformIO extension, Wokwi extension.

```bash
pio run
```

Then run **Wokwi: Start Simulator** in VS Code. Click the DS18B20 to change the temperature and click keypad keys to test.

## Project structure

```
src/main.c        application code
platformio.ini    PlatformIO config (ESP-IDF framework)
diagram.json      Wokwi circuit
wokwi.toml        Wokwi firmware paths
```

## Tools

ESP-IDF, FreeRTOS, PlatformIO, Wokwi